// The iOS counterpart of `android/local.properties`. Copy to `Secrets.xcconfig`
// (gitignored) and fill in. `Debug.xcconfig` and `Release.xcconfig` pull it in
// with `#include?`, so a missing file leaves the value empty and the build still
// runs — the same forgiving behaviour as Gradle's `?: ""` on the Android side.

// The Maps SDK key, read by AppDelegate.swift via the `GMSApiKey` Info.plist
// entry. **Not the Android key, and not the customer app's iOS key either.**
// Google restricts an iOS key by bundle identifier, so this one must be
// restricted to `com.siteonlab.zopiqRider` and the customer's to
// `com.siteonlab.zopiqnow` — two keys from the same Cloud project
// (`789936942272`), exactly as the two apps already carry two Android keys.
//
// "Maps SDK for iOS" must be enabled on the project; it is a different API from
// the Android one and is off by default.
//
// Only the map needs a key here. The route is still measured by Ola and the
// rider's position is still Supabase realtime — neither has ever touched a tile
// server, on either platform.
MAPS_API_KEY =

// The **iOS** OAuth client id for Google sign-in -- not the Android one, and not
// the customer app's. `Env.googleWebClientId` stays exactly as it is: that is the
// `serverClientId`, and it is platform-independent. This is the separate client
// that identifies *this* app to Google.
//
// Create it as an "iOS" client in Cloud project `789936942272` with bundle id
// `com.siteonlab.zopiqRider`. It has no client secret; iOS clients are public by design.
// OAUTH_SETUP_VENDOR_RIDER.md registers only Android clients -- this is the one
// it never covered.
GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID =

// The same client id with its dot-separated components reversed -- Google prints
// it as "iOS URL scheme" on the client's own page, so copy it rather than
// deriving it. It becomes a CFBundleURLScheme, which is how Safari hands the
// sign-in result back to the app.
//
// Looks like: com.googleusercontent.apps.789936942272-abc123def456
GOOGLE_IOS_URL_SCHEME =
